    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good high-level starting point, but I need more granular detail for our assessment scorecard. Our clients want to know what these traits actually look like in practice during a boardroom presentation or a high-stakes negotiation. \n\nCan you get more specific about the non-verbal cues and specific behavioral markers of \"executive presence\"? For example, how should they carry themselves, how should they use their voice, and what kind of social energy should they project to signal they are truly in command of the room? We need to be able to train our recruiters to spot these subtle indicators of leadership gravitas.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Understood. To assess executive presence and commanding authority during a boardroom presentation or negotiation, recruiters should look for the following detailed non-verbal cues and behavioral markers:\n\n1. Body Language: The candidate should have an open, strong posture, standing with their feet roughly shoulder-width apart, and making eye contact to appear approachable yet confident. They should use gestures to emphasize key points but avoid fidgeting or closed-off movements.\n\n2. Posture and Movement: Movements should be purposeful and fluid, yet not too animated. Excessive arm movements or leaning may come across as restless, while a standstill, especially in the middle of a room, could signal discomfort or disengagement.\n\n3. Hand Signs: Confident leaders use hand signs to underline important statements. The movements should be controlled and deliberate, avoiding overly animated gestures.\n\n4. Facial Expressions: Mirroring appropriate emotions depends on the context; however, a candidate should not over-express or seem one-note. Their facial expressions should change subtly and correspond to the content of their speech.\n\n5. Quantity & Quality of Vocal Delivery: They should modulate their tone to keep the room engaged, varying pitch within a ten-to-one range without going over one percent of hissy fits or singing.",
      "source": "target",
